    "hack": {
      "word": "HACK",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "A frame or grating of various kinds; as, a frame for drying bricks, fish, or cheese; a rack for feeding cattle; a grating in a mill race, etc.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "Unburned brick or tile, stacked up for drying.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "To cut irregulary, without skill or definite purpose; to notch; to mangle by repeated strokes of a cutting instrument; as, to hack a post. My sword hacked like a handsaw. Shak.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "Fig.: To mangle in speaking. Shak.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To cough faintly and frequently, or in a short, broken manner; as, a hacking cough.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "A notch; a cut. Shak.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "An implement for cutting a notch; a large pick used in breaking stone.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A hacking; a catch in speaking; a short, broken cough. Dr. H. More.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Football)",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A kick on the shins. T. Hughes. Hack saw, a handsaw having a narrow blade stretched in an iron frame, for cutting metal.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A horse, hackneyed or let out for common hire; also, a horse used in all kinds of work, or a saddle horse, as distinguished from hunting and carriage horses.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A coach or carriage let for hire; particularly, a a coach with two seats inside facing each other; a hackney coach. On horse, on foot, in hacks and gilded chariots. Pope.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A bookmaker who hires himself out for any sort of literary work; an overworked man; a drudge. Here lies poor Ned Purdon, from misery freed, Who long was a bookseller's hack. Goldsmith.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A procuress.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "Hackneyed; hired; mercenary. Wakefield. Hack writer, a hack; one who writes for hire. \"A vulgar hack writer.\" Macaulay.",
          "partOfSpeech": "adjective"
        },
        {
          "text": "To use as a hack; to let out for hire.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To use frequently and indiscriminately, so as to render trite and commonplace. The word \"remarkable\" has been so hacked of late. J. H. Newman.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To be exposed or offered or to common use for hire; to turn prostitute. Hanmer.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To live the life of a drudge or hack. Goldsmith.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        }
      ],
